NAME
       sh - the POSIX shell

SYNOPSIS
       sh
       sh -c 'command'
       sh [-eunx] script [argument ...]

DESCRIPTION
       A POSIX shell, kept separate from csh(1) so that scripts written
       for sh or bash run unchanged. A file whose first line is
       #!/bin/sh is handed to it automatically once the file is
       executable.

GRAMMAR
       Pipelines with |, lists with ; && || and &, grouping with { }
       and ( ), and the compound commands if/elif/else/fi,
       while/do/done, until/do/done, for/in/do/done and case/in/esac.
       Functions are written name() { ... } and take positional
       parameters of their own.

EXPANSION
       $name and ${name}, with ${name:-word} ${name:=word}
       ${name:+word} ${name:?word} for defaults, ${#name} for the
       length, ${name#pat} ${name##pat} ${name%pat} ${name%%pat} for
       trimming, ${name/pat/repl} ${name//pat/repl} for substitution
       and ${name:offset:length} for a slice.

       $(command) and `command` substitute output, $((expression))
       evaluates integer arithmetic, and * ? [...] match path names.
       Unquoted results are split on IFS.

       The special parameters are $? $$ $! $# $@ $* $- and $0 to $9.

REDIRECTION
       < > >> <> >| and n>&m, plus here-documents with << and <<-.
       A quoted delimiter, as in <<'EOF', turns off expansion in the
       body.

BUILTINS
       : . source break cd continue echo eval exec exit export false
       getopts hash local pwd read return set shift test [ times trap
       true type umask unset command

OPTIONS
       -c CMD  run CMD and exit
       -s      read commands from standard input
       -e      exit as soon as a command fails
       -n      parse the input but do not run it
       -u      treat an unset parameter as an error
       -x      print each command before running it

       set turns the same letters on with - and off with + while the
       script is running.

INTERACTIVE
       Line editing, history and Tab completion come from the shared
       readline in libcervus. An unfinished compound command keeps
       prompting with > until it is closed. History is kept in
       ~/.sh_history.

SEE ALSO
       csh(1), test(1), echo(1), printf(1)
